{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000119383","module":"turquoise","kme":0.026169,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.842971,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000119383","entrez_id":["5524"],"uniprot":["Q15257"],"ncbi_summary":"Protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A) is one of the four major Ser/Thr phosphatases and is implicated in the negative control of cell growth and division. The core PP2A holoenzyme is a heterotrimer of a structural A subunit, a catalytic C subunit, and one of several regulatory B subunits, which are grouped into distinct families (B/PR55, B'/PR61, B''/PR72) that confer different substrate specificities and localizations. This gene, PTPA, does not encode one of these conventional B-type regulatory subunits. Rather, this PTPA gene encodes a peptidyl-prolyl cis-trans isomerase that acts as a Mg-ion and ATP-dependent activation chaperone, transiently engaging the PP2A A-C core dimer to restore its catalytic activity, including a distinct phosphotyrosyl phosphatase activity. Unlike classic B-family subunits, which dock stably onto the holoenzyme to alter its specificity, PTPA dissociates after activation rather than remaining a permanent structural component. PTPA was historically classified within the B' family, but this reflects an older nomenclature scheme rather than its actual mechanism, which is now understood to be that of a dedicated activator rather than a structural regulatory subunit. Note that PP2A was used as a symbol for this gene in early publications when its distinct chaperone function was not yet clearly distinguished from the holoenzyme it regulates. Alternative splicing results in multiple transcript variants encoding different isoforms. [provided by RefSeq, Aug 2026].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q15257","text":"PPIases accelerate the folding of proteins. It catalyzes the cis-trans isomerization of proline imidic peptide bonds in oligopeptides (By similarity). Acts as a regulatory subunit for serine/threonine-protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A) (PubMed:16916641, PubMed:36073231). Modulates PP2A activity or substrate specificity, probably by inducing a conformational change in the catalytic subunit, a proposed direct target of the PPIase (PubMed:16916641). Can reactivate inactive phosphatase PP2A-phosphatase methylesterase complexes (PP2A(i)) in presence of ATP and Mg(2+) (By similarity). Reversibly stimulates the variable phosphotyrosyl phosphatase activity of PP2A core heterodimer PP2A(D) in presence of ATP and Mg(2+) (in vitro) (PubMed:16916641). The phosphotyrosyl phosphatase activity is dependent of an ATPase activity of the PP2A(D):PPP2R4 complex (PubMed:16916641). Is involved in apoptosis; the function appears to be independent from PP2A (PubMed:17333320)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}